#---
# purpose: "a closure delivered by the mailbox reduces a list, whatever the accumulator turns out to be"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.152. `Enum.reduce/3` threads the reducer through `List.reduce_cont/3`,
# which specializes on the accumulator: the first step carries the literal
# accumulator's own type, every later step carries the reducer's widened return.
# So ONE callable input reaches two activations of one lambda, and the plan has
# to say what that input physically carries.
#
# Each shape below drives that split from a different direction: an int
# accumulator (the literal type and the widened type are both int-shaped), a
# list accumulator (the literal is the empty list and the steps widen away from
# it), and a reducer holding a capture of its own (the mailbox callable is
# itself a closure with a lane, not a bare function). The empty list is the
# control: it never reaches a reducing step at all.
#
# Paper answer.
#
# Each reducer is minted in main/0, sent to this process's own mailbox, and received
# back before it is used. A value that arrives from a mailbox is first-class data at
# the receive site: nothing there names the activation it was minted from. So sum,
# prepend and scaled each reach Enum.reduce/3 as a generic callable, and the input
# position carrying one is a position whose exact layout is not knowable. That is the
# condition under which a generic carrier is necessary rather than merely convenient.
#
#   sum     : {int, int}       -> int        the accumulator starts int and stays int
#   prepend : {int, list(int)} -> list(int)  the accumulator starts [] and widens
#   scaled  : {int, int}       -> int        and carries a lane, the capture scale
#
# The generic carrier stops there. The continuation `List.reduce_cont/3` returns is
# `{:cont, acc}`: the tag is the same ground atom in every arm, so nothing at runtime
# needs to carry it, and the only part that varies is `acc`. The reduce body's
# self-recursive dispatch asks which arm it holds, and field 1 alone answers that --
# `int` on one path, `[]` on the other. A question about one field of a tuple demands
# that field, not the whole tuple, so the continuation travels as one lane with the tag
# erased, and is never boxed.
#
# Enum.reduce([], 7, sum) reaches no reducing step, so its value is the accumulator it
# was given. The four values are 6, 7, [3, 2, 1] and 60.
def main() do
  send(self(), fn (x, acc) -> acc + x end)
  sum = receive do f -> f end
  dbg(Enum.reduce([1, 2, 3], 0, sum))
  dbg(Enum.reduce([], 7, sum))

  send(self(), fn (x, acc) -> [x | acc] end)
  prepend = receive do f -> f end
  dbg(Enum.reduce([1, 2, 3], [], prepend))

  scale = 10
  send(self(), fn (x, acc) -> acc + x * scale end)
  scaled = receive do f -> f end
  dbg(Enum.reduce([1, 2, 3], 0, scaled))
end
